error[fixed_outside_a_group]: `i8[4]` is a C array member, so it stands only as a field of a `record` inside an `extern` group, whose layout a C compiler chose — here no group's record holds it (§4.19)
  at tests/golden/full/fixedbugs-424-a-fixed-array-at-a-function-s-result-is-told-results-hold-none.hero:15:20
     |
  15 | function four() -> i8[4]  #~ fixed_outside_a_group
     |                    ^^^^^
  note: `Tag`'s field `name` is built from it at line 22: write the elements there instead, `name: [a, b, …]`, as many as `i8[4]` says, or, where they are zero, leave `name` out and end that construction with `rest: zero`, which makes zero every field it does not name: no binding, constant, parameter or result holds a fixed array, and `[i8]` is not one

error[fixed_outside_a_group]: `i32[4]` is a C array member, so it stands only as a field of a `record` inside an `extern` group, whose layout a C compiler chose — here no group's record holds it (§4.19)
  at tests/golden/full/fixedbugs-424-a-fixed-array-at-a-function-s-result-is-told-results-hold-none.hero:18:22
     |
  18 | function counts() -> i32[4]  #~ fixed_outside_a_group
     |                      ^^^^^^
  note: where it is meant for a C struct's field, that struct is a `record` inside the `extern` group of its header, and the array is written out where the record is built, as many elements as `i32[4]` says, `R(field: [a, b, …])`, or, where they are zero, left out of a construction that ends with `rest: zero`, `R(rest: zero)`: no binding, constant, parameter or result holds a fixed array, and `[i32]` is not one
  note: where no C struct holds it, use `[i32]`, built as `[a, b, c]` or grown with `push`: it grows, it is compared and hashed like any other value, and it is what every Heroes record holds
